A||N||4||CozmoPooky||O2 sensor ejection...||||5||
Z||000000||CozmoPooky||12-06-2000||08:13 AM||||Yesterday I was driving in my '87 auto duke coupe when it suddenly sounded like someone yanked the mufflers off!(boom--grrowl) I pulled over and discovered that the O2 sensor decided to "escape" it's hole in the exhaust manifold and was dangling by the wire. I jammed it back in and got home, upon further investigation I found all the threads stripped off from the old sensor. I tried to install a new sensor, but the manifold sensor hole threads were all boogered up with residue from the old sensor. I tried clearing the threads in the manifold with a pointy object, but it's hard to get in there and see what's going on. The sensor seems to be bronze or brass, tapered 1/2" pipe thread. I think the manifold is Stainless. <BR>Can I use a pipe thread cutter and re-tap the manifold to clear the threads? (I see visions of fracturing the manifold...baad). Any suggestions? I also realize that this was probably caused by a plugged cat, I guess that it's time for a "test pipe". <BR>BTW, the ole duke sounded pretty cool for a change  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>Bob Cardone (CozmoPooky)<BR>'87 Coupe<BR>'85 2M6 Targa<p>[This message has been edited by CozmoPooky (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-06-2000||08:32 AM||||YES, IF THE THREADS ARE JUST 'DIRTY' YOU CAN RE-TAP THE HOLE. THE OTHER PART IS THAT IF IT BLEW OUT, THE EXHAUST IS CLOGGED AS WELL. JUST A TEST PIPE WON'T CUT IT, YOU'LL NEED A NEW MUFFLER AS WELL.<P>Sorry about the caps!||||reg||1
Z||000002||PlasticWrapd||12-06-2000||08:59 AM||||Hate to say it,but chances are the threads are stripped.  If the sensor blew out, then both sets of threads are probably damaged.  Save yourself the trouble now and repair the threads.  K&D makes a repair kit for sparkplugs and o2 sensor threads.  Comes with an easy to install insert and a tap.  Much easier than Helicoil and the insert is pretty tough.  It's a solid insert, unlike Helicoil which is spring-like.||||reg||14
Z||000003||theogre||12-06-2000||07:28 PM||||O2 sensor IS Not a NPT tapered thread. It's a straight thread. You'll most likey need the insert kit mentioned above.||||reg||1
Z||000004||CozmoPooky||12-06-2000||07:56 PM||||Sounds like a new exhaust system||||reg||1
